    "result_text": "Done. The change is complete and the full test suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`, 2/2 tests passing).\n\n## What I did\n\nThe codebase already establishes a clear pattern for read-only *derived* owner fields (`membershipNumber`, `membershipTier`, `displayName`, `initials`): a computed getter on the domain model, declared as `readOnly` in `openapi.yml`, which MapStruct auto-maps onto the generated `OwnerDto`. I followed that same pattern for `locality`:\n\n1. **`Owner.java`** \u2014 added `getLocality()`, deriving the region from the owner's own `city` field via a fixed `CITY_REGIONS` table (`Sydney\u2192NSW, Melbourne\u2192VIC, Brisbane\u2192QLD`), returning `\"UNKNOWN\"` for anything else:\n   ```java\n   private static final Map<String, String> CITY_REGIONS =\n       Map.of(\"Sydney\", \"NSW\", \"Melbourne\", \"VIC\", \"Brisbane\", \"QLD\");\n\n   public String getLocality() {\n       return CITY_REGIONS.getOrDefault(this.city, \"UNKNOWN\");\n   }\n   ```\n\n2. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added the `locality` property (readOnly) to the `Owner` schema, so the generated DTO exposes it and MapStruct wires `owner.getLocality()` \u2192 `dto.locality` with no mapper changes needed.\n\nThe logic lives with the field it derives from (consistent with the existing `membershipTier`/`displayName` derived getters), stays a small single-purpose unit, and adds no new catch-all class.",
